findings.body:
`logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)` was placed between `import prefect` and the `from prefect._sdk.models import (...)` block. This makes the subsequent imports non-top-level, so ruff reports E402 (module-level import not at top of file). Import sorting (the `I` rule, which the project extends) also fails. The base commit lints clean, and head fails with four E402 errors in `src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py`.

::diff{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="17-22"}

Move the logger definition below the last import, as the base commit did, so the imports stay together at the top and CI lint stays green.

`zip(pool_names_list, results, strict=True)` was relaxed to `zip(pool_names_list, results)`. Both lists come from the same comprehension, so their lengths always match and this is not a live bug. But `strict=True` was an explicit guard against silent truncation. Dropping it without a reason weakens the invariant, and the project targets Python 3.10+, where `strict` is available.

::diff{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="219-221"}

Restore `strict=True`.
